WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
Question 0039: eggs and floors,
a junior-level question.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:09.900
Two parts: three eggs and a hundred floors,
then k eggs and n floors, with code.

3
00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:15.300
A building of 100 floors, and three identical eggs.
The building has a threshold floor.

4
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:19.833
An egg dropped above the threshold breaks,
and from the threshold or below it does not.

5
00:00:19.833 --> 00:00:23.833
The threshold can be any number from 0 to 100.
We must find it in the worst case.

6
00:00:24.633 --> 00:00:29.300
Part A: how many drops in the worst case,
and from which floors do we drop?

7
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:35.467
With one egg there is no choice:
drop from floor 1, 2, 3, and so on.

8
00:00:35.467 --> 00:00:40.700
If it breaks midway, there is no other egg.
So in the worst case: 100 drops.

9
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:46.367
With two eggs, the first one jumps,
and the second goes floor by floor below it.

10
00:00:46.367 --> 00:00:51.900
The jumps shrink by one: 14, 27, 39.
Every jump costs the first egg a drop.

11
00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:57.400
If the first breaks at 27, the second checks
15 to 26: 2 + 12 = 14 drops.

12
00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:01.400
Wherever the first one breaks, it comes to 14.
That is the worst case with two eggs.

13
00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:07.200
The idea: turn the question around. How many floors
can k eggs and t drops settle?

14
00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:12.700
Drop from one floor. If it broke, the threshold is below,
with one egg fewer and one drop fewer.

15
00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:16.867
If it did not break, the threshold is above:
the same eggs, and one drop fewer.

16
00:01:16.867 --> 00:01:22.167
Plus the floor itself. That is the recurrence,
with zero when there are no eggs or no drops.

17
00:01:22.967 --> 00:01:27.567
Fill a table: one egg settles t floors,
and two settle t(t + 1)/2.

18
00:01:27.567 --> 00:01:33.300
The three-egg column: every cell is
the cell up-left, plus 1, plus the cell above.

19
00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:38.200
For example 129 = 36 + 1 + 92.
With 8 drops only 92 floors, fewer than 100.

20
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:42.200
And with 9 drops already 129 floors.
So the answer is 9 drops.

21
00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:49.100
The first drop: if it broke, two eggs
and 8 drops are left for 36 floors. So from floor 37.

22
00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:54.067
If it did not break, the next jump is smaller:
to floor 66, then 88, then 100.

23
00:01:55.467 --> 00:01:59.767
Let us run the plan on the page's example,
with the threshold at floor 36.

24
00:01:59.767 --> 00:02:04.567
37 is above the threshold: the egg breaks.
Two eggs and eight drops are left for 1 to 36.

25
00:02:04.567 --> 00:02:10.467
The second in shrinking jumps: 8, 15, 21, 26,
30, 33, 35. None of them breaks.

26
00:02:10.467 --> 00:02:15.700
The ninth drop, from floor 36, does not break.
36 is safe and 37 breaks: the threshold is 36.

27
00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:20.600
The page's box runs the same example,
drop after drop.

28
00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:24.700
Drop 1 from floor 37: broke.
The part above 36 is known to break.

29
00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:29.167
Drop 2 from floor 8: did not break.
The unknown range is 9 to 36.

30
00:02:29.167 --> 00:02:34.967
Then 15, 21 and 26: the unknown range
shrinks with every drop, from below.

31
00:02:34.967 --> 00:02:40.767
And after them 30, 33 and 35.
None of them breaks.

32
00:02:40.767 --> 00:02:45.567
And in the ninth drop, from floor 36: the threshold is 36.
Nine drops, as computed.

33
00:02:46.367 --> 00:02:51.600
And why not 8? Eight drops and three eggs
tell apart at most 93 thresholds.

34
00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:56.133
And there are 101 possible thresholds, from 0 to 100.
The full proof is in part B.

35
00:02:56.933 --> 00:03:02.433
The number of drops grows like the cube root of 6n:
19 for 1,000 floors, and 40 for 10,000.

36
00:03:03.733 --> 00:03:07.633
A trap: binary search. From floor 50,
and the egg breaks.

37
00:03:07.633 --> 00:03:12.933
Two eggs are left for 49 floors, which need
10 drops. 11 in all, not 9.

38
00:03:13.728 --> 00:03:18.394
Part B: any number of eggs and any number of floors.
A formula, a proof and code.

39
00:03:19.094 --> 00:03:23.561
The recurrence has a closed form:
a sum of binomial coefficients.

40
00:03:23.561 --> 00:03:28.861
The proof is by induction, with Pascal's rule.
For three eggs and 9 drops: 129.

41
00:03:29.661 --> 00:03:35.194
Every plan is a decision tree: at each node
a drop, left if it broke and right if not.

42
00:03:35.194 --> 00:03:40.428
Every path has at most k breaks.
With two eggs and three drops, 000 is impossible.

43
00:03:40.428 --> 00:03:45.094
So at most 7 leaves, and 7 thresholds:
exactly 6 floors, f(2, 3).

44
00:03:45.894 --> 00:03:50.794
In general: a leaf per threshold, so we need
f(k, t) ≥ n. The condition the code looks for.

45
00:03:50.794 --> 00:03:56.194
With three eggs and 8 drops: 93 leaves,
and there are 101 thresholds. So 8 is not enough.

46
00:03:56.994 --> 00:03:59.994
Now let us write it in Python.

47
00:04:03.228 --> 00:04:07.728
egg_table builds the table F: how many floors
e eggs settle in t drops.

48
00:04:10.661 --> 00:04:15.161
At first everything is zero: with no eggs
or no drops no floor is settled.

49
00:04:22.494 --> 00:04:26.994
For every e and every t, the recurrence of part A,
capped at n: above n the value changes nothing.

50
00:04:27.894 --> 00:04:30.394
And returns the table.

51
00:04:38.194 --> 00:04:42.661
min_drops: the first t
at which the table covers all n floors.

52
00:04:47.428 --> 00:04:51.928
egg_run runs the plan against a given threshold,
and returns the drops.

53
00:04:58.228 --> 00:05:02.728
low: the highest floor known to be safe. high:
the lowest floor known to break. Plus eggs and t.

54
00:05:05.194 --> 00:05:09.161
As long as some floor between low and high
is unknown, we drop.

55
00:05:13.494 --> 00:05:17.994
The next floor: above low by exactly what
the eggs left can settle, plus one.

56
00:05:22.694 --> 00:05:26.728
Above the threshold the egg breaks.
We record the drop and its result.

57
00:05:28.294 --> 00:05:30.961
Either way one drop fewer is left.

58
00:05:36.361 --> 00:05:40.861
Broke: high comes down to the floor, and one egg fewer.
Not broken: low goes up to the floor.

59
00:05:42.794 --> 00:05:47.294
When high = low + 1 no floor is unknown,
and we return the drops.

60
00:05:48.494 --> 00:05:53.094
Let us run it on the page's example: three eggs,
100 floors, and the threshold 36.

61
00:05:53.094 --> 00:05:58.694
The first drop, from floor 37, is above the threshold:
it broke. high = 37, and two eggs are left.

62
00:05:58.694 --> 00:06:03.261
The second: 0 + F[1][7] + 1 = 8.
It did not break, so low = 8.

63
00:06:03.261 --> 00:06:07.761
Then six drops that do not break:
15, 21, 26, 30, 33 and 35.

64
00:06:07.761 --> 00:06:12.328
The ninth from floor 36: did not break.
low = 36, and high = 37.

65
00:06:12.328 --> 00:06:17.294
No floor is unknown any more: the threshold is 36,
after nine drops, as in part A.

66
00:06:18.094 --> 00:06:23.194
The box runs the plan against every threshold,
from 0 to 100, and counts the drops.

67
00:06:23.194 --> 00:06:27.194
No threshold goes past 9, and 46 thresholds
reach exactly 9: that is the worst case.

68
00:06:27.994 --> 00:06:33.394
The cost: the table O(kn), one run O(t),
and the check against every threshold O(nt).

69
00:06:34.194 --> 00:06:39.694
A trap: counting 100 thresholds. The threshold can
also be 0 or 100: there are n + 1 thresholds.

70
00:06:39.694 --> 00:06:43.194
Whoever counts n gets a lower bound
that is off by one.

71
00:06:43.985 --> 00:06:48.218
To sum up: instead of how many drops, how many floors
t drops settle.

72
00:06:48.218 --> 00:06:53.618
Try your own numbers in the box
on the page of question 0039 on the site.
